$\begin{align}
b^4\cdot b&=b^4\cdot b^1
\\\\
&=b^{4+1}
\\\\
&=b^{5}
\end{align}$

This follows from the general rule $x^m\cdot x^n=x^{m+n}$. Note that the powers have the same base.

We can also see this is correct by expanding the powers.

$\begin{align}
b^4\cdot b&=\underbrace{b\cdot b\cdot b\cdot b}_\text{4 times}\cdot\underbrace{b}_\text{1 time}
\\\\\\
&=\underbrace{b\cdot b\cdot b\cdot b\cdot b}_\text{5 times}
\\\\
&=b^{5}
\end{align}$
